Frontend with Streamlit, explains the use of","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 6, Building an AI LLM Network Application Frontend with Streamlit, explains the use of\nStreamlit to create a simple web interface for AI Network Applications.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":23,"pageEnd":23,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::20","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"Chapter 7, Building AI LLM Application Backends, covers the creation, use, and sharing of AI LLM","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 7, Building AI LLM Application Backends, covers the creation, use, and sharing of AI LLM\napplications with basic API servers and backend to service our applications.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":23,"pageEnd":23,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::21","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"Chapter 8, Building a Network Co-Pilot, illustrates the building core pieces of building a network","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 8, Building a Network Co-Pilot, illustrates the building core pieces of building a network\nassistant to help with our network engineering tasks.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":23,"pageEnd":23,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::22","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"Chapter 9, Network Monitoring and Performance Use Cases with MCP, provides a starting point","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 9, Network Monitoring and Performance Use Cases with MCP, provides a starting point\nfor practical approach to AI-powered network monitoring data, leveraging the Model Context\nProtocol (MCP).","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":23,"pageEnd":23,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::23","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"Chapter 10, Network Security through Vibe Coding, explains how to use coding assistant to help","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 10, Network Security through Vibe Coding, explains how to use coding assistant to help\nwith our network security posture.\nTo get the most out of this book\n• The reader should have a basic understanding of networking concepts and principals.\n• Basic understanding of programming language of Python will be beneficial.\nDownload the example code files\nThe code bundle for the book is hosted on GitHub at https://github.com/PacktPublishing/\nAI-Networking-Cookbook-First-Edition. We also have other code bundles from our rich cat-\nalog of books and videos available at https://github.com/PacktPublishing. Check them out!\nPreface xxiii\nConventions used\nThere are a number of text conventions used throughout this book.\nCodeInText: Indicates code words in text, database table names, folder names, filenames, file\nextensions, pathnames, dummy URLs, user input, and Twitter handles. For example: “Use the\nfollowing docker-compose.yml file to specify the two containers we will download, one for hous -\ning models named ollama, and ollama-webui as the web interface:”\nA block of code is set as follows:\ndef prepare_advanced_input(input_dict):\nreturn {\n\"config\": input_dict[\"config\"],\n\"basic_analysis\": input_dict[\"basic_analysis\"]\n}\nAny command-line input or output is written as follows:\npip install openai python-dotenv\nBold: Indicates a new term, an important word, or words that you see on the screen. For instance,\nwords in menus or dialog boxes appear in the text like this. For example: “Click on the top navi-\ngation menu on the left-hand side, choose Workspaces, and choose to create a new workspace.”\nGet in touch\nFeedback from our readers is always welcome.\nGeneral feedback: If you have questions about any aspect of this book or have any general feed-\nback, please email us at customercare@packt.com and mention the book’s title in the subject\nof your message.\nWarnings or important notes appear like this.\nTips and tricks appear like this.\nPreface xxiv\nErrata: Although we have taken every care to ensure the accuracy of our content, mistakes do\nhappen. If you have found a mistake in this book, we would be grateful if you reported this to us.\nPlease visit http://www.packt.com/submit-errata, click Submit Errata, and fill in the form.\nPiracy: If you come across any illegal copies of our works in any form on the internet, we would\nbe grateful if you would provide us with the location address or website name. Please contact us\nat copyright@packt.com with a link to the material.\nIf you are interested in becoming an author: If there is a topic that you have expertise in and you\nare interested in either writing or contributing to a book, please visit http://authors.packt.com/.\nJoin Eric Live for Build Intelligent Networks with AI\nWant to go hands-on with the ideas in this book? Join Eric Chou for a live, online workshop on","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":23,"pageEnd":25,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::24","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"Chapter 10, Network Security through Vibe Coding, explains how to use coding assistant to help","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"January 24, 2026. We’ll dig into network design patterns, real-world demos, and practical Q&A to\nhelp you ship faster, more reliable networks. Seats are limited—secure your spot now: https://\npackt.link/buildnetworksai.\nHands-on workshop in automation and observability\nPreface xxv\nShare your thoughts\nOnce you’ve read AI Networking Cookbook, we’d love to hear your thoughts! Please click here\nto go straight to the Amazon review page for this book and share your feedback.\nYour review is important to us and the tech community and will help us make sure we’re deliv-\nering excellent quality content.\nPreface xxvi\nFree Benefits with Your Book\nThis book comes with free benefits to support your learning. Activate them now for instant access\n(see the “ How to Unlock ” section for instructions).\nHere’s a quick overview of what you can instantly unlock with your purchase:\nPDF and ePub Copies Next-Gen Web-Based Reader\nAccess a DRM-free PDF copy of this book\nto read anywhere, on any device.\nMulti-device progress sync: Pick up\nwhere you left off, on any device.\nUse a DRM-free ePub version with your\nfavorite e-reader.\nHighlighting and notetaking: Capture\nideas and turn reading into lasting\nknowledge.\nBookmarking: Save and revisit key\nsections whenever you need them.\nDark mode: Reduce eye strain by\nswitching to dark or sepia themes.\nPreface xxvii\nHow to Unlock\nScan the QR code (or go to packtpub.com/unlock). Search for this\nbook by name, confirm the edition, and then follow the steps on\nthe page.\nNote: Keep your invoice handy. Purchases made directly from Packt\ndon’t require one.\n1\nThe AI LLM Landscape and Key\nParameters\nAI is changing the landscape of every technology field. Network engineering is no exception.\nSpecifically, we will cover the following recipes in this chapter:\n• Setting up an OpenAI account\n• Experimenting with OpenAI LLM models and model parameters\n• Script with OpenAI\n• Setting up local AI Ollama models\n• Script with Ollama models\nTechnical requirements\nThis chapter requires no prior knowledge of artificial intelligence or large language models. If\nadditional technology is required for a particular recipe, it will be mentioned in the recipe.\nFree Benefits with Your Book\nYour purchase includes a free PDF copy of this book along with other exclusive benefits. Check\nthe Free Benefits with Your Book section in the Preface to unlock them instantly and maximize\nyour learning experience.\nThe AI LLM Landscape and Key Parameters 2","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":25,"pageEnd":31,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::25","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"1.1","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"1.1 Setting up an OpenAI environment","sectionNumber":"1.1","sectionTitle":"Setting up an OpenAI environment","content":"1.1 Setting up an OpenAI environment\nOpenAI is the dominant player when it comes to Large Language Models (LLMs). The ChatGPT\napplication was arguably what kick-started the Artificial Intelligence (AI) movement in 2023,\nreaching 100 million users in only 2 months. Before we can access OpenAI resources, such as ex-\nperimenting with the playground and making API requests, we will need to set up the developer\nenvironment.\nIn this recipe, we will set up the OpenAI environment to start using OpenAI services, including\ngenerating an API key, familiarizing ourselves with the settings page, and other settings.\nGetting ready\nWe will need to create an OpenAI platform account; this is not the same as an OpenAI account. An\nOpenAI account is primarily used for individual users to interact with OpenAI’s AI model through\na web interface such as ChatGPT. An OpenAI platform account, on the other hand, is designed for\ndevelopers to build applications and services that leverage OpenAI’s models.\nNavigate to https://platform.openai.com/ and sign in to your existing OpenAI account. If you\ndo not have an account, you can sign up for a free account with an email address. You can also\nlog in to OpenAI with a valid Google, Microsoft, or Apple account.\nFollow the onscreen instructions to complete the creation of your account with the steps of\nidentity verification and payment setup.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":31,"pageEnd":31,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::26","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"Chapter 1 3","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 1 3\nHow to do it…\n1. Navigate to https://platform.openai.com/settings/organization/ and use the left\nnavigation bar to create API keys, create new projects, check usage, and perform other\nadministrative tasks.\nFigure 1.1 – OpenAI platform settings\n2. Create an organization API key on the https://platform.openai.com/settings/\norganization/api-keys page. Copy the key in a safe place to be used later (by default,\nDefault project is selected; you can also create a new project to be more explicit in your\nconfiguration).\nFigure 1.2 – OpenAI organization API key creation\nThe AI LLM Landscape and Key Parameters 4\n3. Use the top navigation menu or this link to access OpenAI chat playground: https://\nplatform.openai.com/chat/edit?models=gpt-4o.\nFigure 1.3 – OpenAI playground\n4. The first time using it, OpenAI will ask you to set up a credit card on file and a minimum\ncharge of a few dollars ($5 at the time of writing).\n5. Type a question in the chat prompt and observe the answer from OpenAI.\nFigure 1.4 – OpenAI chat conversation","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":32,"pageEnd":33,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::27","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"Chapter 1 5","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 1 5\nPlease note that, unlike the consumer ChatGPT interface, the chat history is not persistent. When\nwe refresh the page, the previous history will be lost.\nHow it works…\nThe playground and API are two of the key components we will use in our development process.\nThe playground is where we can learn and experiment with different model settings, parame-\nters, and results. As shown in Figure 1.5, the playground interface consists of model parameters,\nsystem messages, chat input, and the conversation being displayed. The web interface is a quick\nand easy way to optimize the prompt and other settings.\nFigure 1.5 – OpenAI playground web interface\nOnce we are happy with the result, we can use the API key to programmatically interact with\nOpenAI models using certain parameters, as we will see in later recipes.\nThe AI LLM Landscape and Key Parameters 6\nThere’s more…\nThe OpenAI platform is constantly changing, with new parameters, models, and guidelines. Nav-\nigate to the FAQ page, https://platform.openai.com/docs/faq, for commonly asked questions.\nFeel free to play around with different conversation prompts and answers generated by OpenAI.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":34,"pageEnd":35,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::28","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"1.2","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"1.2 Experiment with OpenAI LLM models and model","sectionNumber":"1.2","sectionTitle":"Experiment with OpenAI LLM models and model","content":"1.2 Experiment with OpenAI LLM models and model\nparameters\nThe OpenAI playground allows the user to select different models and tweak the model param-\neters. The models differ in their capabilities and cost. At the time of writing, here are the general\nmodel types:\n• Reasoning models: o-series models that excel at complex, multi-step tasks. Examples:\no4-min, o3-mini, o1-pro.\n• Chat models: Original, versatile, high-intelligence models. Examples: GPT-4.1, GPT-4o.\n• Cost-optimized models: Smaller, faster models that cost less to run. Examples: o4-mini,\nGPT-4.1 mini, GPT-4.1 nana.\n• Real-time models: Models capable of real-time text and audio input and outputs.\nThe pricing of the models can be found at https://platform.openai.com/docs/pricing, in-\ncrements of price per 1M tokens.\nGetting ready\nMake sure you are logged in to the OpenAI playground and on the playground interface, as shown\nin Figure 1.5.\nHow to do it…\n1. We can begin by selecting the model we want to use, with the drop-down Model menu.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":35,"pageEnd":35,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::29","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"Chapter 1 7","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 1 7\nLet’s select gpt-4o to start:\nFigure 1.6 – gpt-4o prompt\nFigure 1.7 – gpt-4o prompt\nThe AI LLM Landscape and Key Parameters 8\n2. Type in the question How to configure VLAN on a Cisco IOS-XE device? and observe\nthe response.\nFigure 1.8 – VLAN configuration response\n3. Pick a different model, such as gpt-4 � 1-nano, ask the same question – How to configure\nVLAN on a Cisco IOS-XE device? – and observe the response.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":36,"pageEnd":37,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::30","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"Chapter 1 9","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 1 9\n4. We can also see the response time difference between the two models. In my run, gpt-4.1-\nnano generated a response in 1.542ms, while gpt-4o took 5.575ms for the same question,\nso the nano model was more than twice as fast.\nFigure 1.9 – VLAN configuration response with gpt-4.1-nano\n5. Use the modal selector to see the Temperature, Max tokens, and Top P sliders.\nFigure 1.10 – Temperature, Max tokens, and Top P options\nThe AI LLM Landscape and Key Parameters 10\n6. The Temperature parameter controls the creativity or randomness of the AI response. The\nhigher the temperature, the less deterministic and more creative the response. We can\ntry dialing up Temperature all the way to 2 and ask the same question. As you can see in\nFigure 1.11, the response was unrecognizable, even with foreign characters.\nFigure 1.11 – Response with Option Tweaks\n7. Change Temperature back to 1 and lower Max tokens down to 20. If we ask the same\nquestion, we can see the response is really short. Max tokens determines the length of\nthe response the model can return.\nFigure 1.12 – Max tokens option response","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":38,"pageEnd":39,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::31","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"Chapter 1 11","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 1 11\n8. Finally, let’s tweak Top P from 1 to 0.25. The setting determines the “diversity” of the\nresponse. The lower the value, the more deterministic and less diverse the response.\nHow it works…\nOpenAI’s text generation models have been trained to understand natural language and pro-\nvide text outputs in response to their inputs. The models use probability from their training to\ndetermine the type of response they should return. The parameters we covered in this recipe are\nwhat we can use to influence the returned text and how deterministic or creative they should be.\nThere’s more…\nTake a look at the Advanced usage page for the parameter details, managing tokens, and repro-\nducible outputs: https://platform.openai.com/docs/guides/advanced-usage.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":40,"pageEnd":40,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::32","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"1.3","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"1.3 Experimenting with OpenAI System message","sectionNumber":"1.3","sectionTitle":"Experimenting with OpenAI System message","content":"1.3 Experimenting with OpenAI System message\nLocated below the parameters is a System message box. By default, there are grayed-out words\nstating Describe desired model behavior (tone, tool usage, and response style). This is one of\nthe first places we can influence how OpenAI interacts with the user by providing desired guide -\nlines, tone, and objectives. It is where we can describe the desired model behavior, such as the\ntone and response style – for example, answer as if you are a senior network engineer talking to\nan intelligent fifth-grader.\nGetting ready\nLocate the System message box from Figure 1.5 before proceeding with the examples.\nHow to do it…\n1. Let’s begin with the following system message:\nYou are an AI assistant specialized in network engineering. Your role is\nto assist with tasks, provide explanations, and offer guidance related to\nnetwork engineering concepts, protocols, tools, and troubleshooting.\nThe AI LLM Landscape and Key Parameters 12\n2. For the user prompt, let’s ask OpenAI What are the steps to set up WiFi 7? and\nobserve the response.\nFigure 1.13 – OpenAI System message\n3. Let’s modify the system message to the following:\nYou are an AI assistant 5th grade teacher specialized in network engineering.\nYour role is to assist with tasks, provide explanations, and offer guidance\nrelated to network engineering concepts, protocols, tools, and troubleshooting\nand explain them to elementary school kids.\nThe instructions for the interaction are as follows:\n• Provide funny explanations whenever necessary\n• Use non-technical language","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":40,"pageEnd":41,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::33","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"Chapter 1 13","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 1 13\n4. We can ask the same question, What are the steps to set up WiFi 7?, and observe\nthe response:\nFigure 1.14 – OpenAI System message experiment\n5. Some of the additional changes we can make in System message include the following:\n• Provide the answer in a paragraph format\n• Provide the answer in a YAML format\n• Provide the answer in a JSON format\nFigure 1.15 – Assistant response with System message\nThe AI LLM Landscape and Key Parameters 14\nLet’s dive deeper into how it works.\nHow it works…\nThe system message can influence the response from OpenAI for the desired output. Here are a\nfew key concepts for system messages:\n• Set the right context: Provide background information or a description of tasks – for\nexample, “you are an AI assistant specialized in network engineering.”\n• Provide clear instructions: Use clear, concise, and unambiguous language and be explicit\nabout what you want the model to do. For example, “Your role is to assist with tasks, pro-\nvide explanations, and offer guidance related to network engineering concepts, protocols,\ntools, and troubleshooting and explain them to elementary school kids.”\n• Specify output formatting: Specify how you want the output to be structured – for ex-\nample, “... provide the answer in YAML format. ”\nThere are other prompt engineering methods we will get into in other recipes; this recipe provides\na good start.\nThere’s more…\nOpenAI prompt engineering guide, at https://platform.openai.com/docs/guides/text?api-\nmode=responses#prompt-engineering, provides excellent guides as a starting point for prompt\nengineering for system messages.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":42,"pageEnd":43,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::34","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"1.4","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"1.4 Experimenting with OpenAI prompt engineering","sectionNumber":"1.4","sectionTitle":"Experimenting with OpenAI prompt engineering","content":"1.4 Experimenting with OpenAI prompt engineering\nin chat\nThe same concept can be used in OpenAI chat for prompt engineering. Unlike the system mes-\nsage, the prompt we use only impacts the current conversation and will be cleared if we start a\nnew conversation.\nGetting ready\nNo other preparation is required besides using the chat box.\nHow to do it…\n1. Make sure the system message is clear of any instructions.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":43,"pageEnd":43,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::35","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"Chapter 1 15","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 1 15\n2. Enter the same instruction as in the last recipe, but now in the chat box:\nYou are an AI assistant 5th grade teacher specialized in network engineering.\nYour role is to assist with tasks, provide explanations, and offer guidance\nrelated to network engineering concepts, protocols, tools, and troubleshooting\nand explain them to elementary school kids.\nInstructions for the interaction are as follows:\n• Provide funny explanations whenever necessary\n• Use non-technical language\n• Provide the answer in a paragraph format\nFigure 1.16 – Experiment with OpenAI prompt engineering\n3. Observe the output; they should be similar. However, when we enter a new chat with\nauto-clear on, the previous instruction will be cleared.\nHow it works…\nThe same prompt engineering concept we observed in the last recipe can be applied in the chat\nconversation. The difference is that the instructions are not persistent between chats.\nThere’s more…\nThe OpenAI prompt engineering guide, at https://platform.openai.com/docs/guides/\ntext?api-mode=responses#prompt-engineering, provides excellent guides as a starting point\nfor prompt engineering for system messages.\nThe AI LLM Landscape and Key Parameters 16","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":44,"pageEnd":45,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::36","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"1.5","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"1.5 Python scripts with OpenAI","sectionNumber":"1.5","sectionTitle":"Python scripts with OpenAI","content":"1.5 Python scripts with OpenAI\nWe can also use Python to interact with the OpenAI ChatGPT model.\nGetting ready\nLocate the OpenAI key from the previous recipe, as we will use it in our script. Create a Python\nvirtual environment to work with the following script:\n$ python3 -m venv .venv\n$ source .venv/bin/activate\nExport your OpenAI key as an environment variable.\n$ export OPENAI_API_KEY=\"your-openai-api-key\"\nHow to do it…\n1. Install the openai Python package.\n$ pip install openai\n2. Construct the Python script, ch01_hello_world.py:\nimport os\nfrom openai import OpenAI\nclient = OpenAI(\napi_key=os.environ.get(\"OPENAI_API_KEY\"),\n)\ncompletion = client.chat.completions.create(\nmodel=\"gpt-4o\",\nmessages=[\n{\n\"role\": \"developer\",\n\"content\": \"You are an AI assistant specialized in\nnetwork engineering..\"\n},\n{\n\"role\": \"user\",","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":45,"pageEnd":45,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::37","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"Chapter 1 17","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 1 17\n\"content\": \"How do I install WiFi 7?\",\n},\n],\n)\nprint(completion.choices[0].message.content)\n3. Observe the output:\n$ python ch01_hello_world.py\nWiFi 7, also known as IEEE 802.11be, is the next generation of\nWiFi technology designed to provide faster data rates, improved\nefficiency, and enhanced capacity. As of my last update in October\n2023, WiFi 7 was in development and might not yet be widely\navailable in consumer products. However, if you're looking to\ninstall WiFi 7 when compatible equipment becomes available, here are\nthe general steps you would follow:\n1. **Check for Compatibility:**\n- Ensure that your devices (router, access points, and client\ndevices like smartphones, tablets, and laptops) support WiFi 7. You\nmay need to upgrade your hardware if it isn't compatible.\n…\nLet’s break down the script and see how it works.\nHow it works…\nIn the script, we use the os module to access the environment variable for the OpenAI API key, as\nwell as importing the openai Python library, and initiating the client object.\nThe client.chat.completion.create method is sent to OpenAI’s endpoint to generate a response.\nThe role is similar to the system message we saw before in the previous recipe; the user is the chat\nmessage we sent to OpenAI.\nThere’s more…\nThe OpenAI Python API library, at https://github.com/openai/openai-python, provides more\nexamples of the Python library.\nThe AI LLM Landscape and Key Parameters 18","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":46,"pageEnd":47,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::38","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"1.6","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"1.6 Local AI model Ollama","sectionNumber":"1.6","sectionTitle":"Local AI model Ollama","content":"1.6 Local AI model Ollama\nOllama is an easy way to experiment with running a language model locally.\nGetting ready\nEnsure you have an Ollama account. Sign up at https://ollama.com/signup if you need to. Also,\nmake sure you have Docker Engine (https://www.docker.com/) and docker-compose (https://\ndocs.docker.com/compose/) installed on the machine you wish to run the images on.\nHow to do it…\nThe Ollama site provides easy-to-download Docker images that can switch between models easily.\n1. Launch Docker engine, such as with docker.desktop.\n2. Write the docker-compose file with the text editor, and name it docker-compose.yml.\nnetworks:\nollama:\nservices:\nollama:\nimage: ollama/ollama\nnetworks:\n- ollama\n# deploy:\n# resources:\n# reservations:\n# devices:\n# - driver: nvidia\n# count: 1\n# capabilities: [ gpu ]\nvolumes:\n- ./data/ollama:/root/.ollama\nports:\n- 11434:11434\nollama-webui:\nimage: ghcr.io/ollama-webui/ollama-webui:main\nvolumes:","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":47,"pageEnd":47,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::39","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"Chapter 1 19","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 1 19\n- ./data/ollama-webui:/app/backend/data\ndepends_on:\n- ollama\nports:\n- 3002:8080\nenvironment:\n- 'OLLAMA_API_BASE_URL=http://ollama:11434/api'\nextra_hosts:\n- host.docker.internal:host-gateway\nnetworks:\n- Ollamaollama\n3. Launch the Docker images and custom network with the docker-compose command:\n$ docker-compose up\n✔ ollama-webui Pulled\n30.0s\n✔ f546e941f15b Pull complete\n2.3s\n✔ 24935aba99a7 Pull complete\n2.5s\n…\n✔ ollama Pulled\n115.7s\n✔ 1b9f3c55f9d4 Pull complete\n14.9s\n✔ Network ollama-docker_ollama Created\n0.0s\n✔ Container ollama-docker-ollama-1 Created\n0.3s\n✔ Container ollama-docker-ollama-webui-1 Created\n0.0s\nollama-webui-1 | INFO: Application startup complete.\nollama-webui-1 | INFO: Uvicorn running on http://0.0.0.0:8080\n(Press CTRL+C to quit)\nThe AI LLM Landscape and Key Parameters 20\n4. Access the Ollama WebUI with http ://localhost:3002:\nFigure 1.17 – Ollama WebUI\n5. Use the Sign up link to create a local account. The initial account will have administrative\naccess.\nFigure 1.18 – Ollama Web UI","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":48,"pageEnd":49,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::40","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"Chapter 1 21","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 1 21\n6. Use http ://localhost:11434/ to verify Ollama is running.\nFigure 1.19 – Ollama localhost\n7. Click on the settings gear to launch the settings menu.\nFigure 1.20 – Ollama home screen\nThe AI LLM Landscape and Key Parameters 22\n8. Choose Models, type in gemma3:1b, and click on the green download button to pull the\nmodel from Ollama.\nFigure 1.21 – Ollama model selection\n9. Once the model finishes downloading, we can choose the model from a list of dropdowns.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":50,"pageEnd":51,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::41","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"Chapter 1 23","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 1 23\n10. Ask a question in the chat box, such as What is Layer 1 of the OSI model?\nFigure 1.22 – Ollama response\nLet’s see how it works.\nThe AI LLM Landscape and Key Parameters 24\nHow it works…\nThere are two Docker images involved in this recipe, ollama and ollama-webui. The ollama image\nis where the model is located, while ollama-webui provides a management interface to easily\nmanage the model download and a chat frontend for the setup.\nOut of the box, there is no model installed on the Docker image; we need to search and install\na model. We picked the gemma3:1b model because it is relatively small to download while still\nbeing reasonable in its performance. There are so many different models to pick from; we can\nrely on the ranking of the model as well as the model description to start picking the model we\nshould work with. From the web page, we can evaluate the model based on the number of pulls,\nthe size of the model, and the number of parameters.\nFigure 1.23 – Model evaluation","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":52,"pageEnd":53,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::42","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"Chapter 1 25","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 1 25\nWe can also scroll down the model page to see the different versions of the model, such as text,\nvision, and others.\nFigure 1.24 – Additional models\nThere’s more…\nCheck out https://ollama.com/ for more information on the models and other ways to launch\nlocal LLM models.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":54,"pageEnd":54,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::43","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"1.7","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"1.7 Python scripts with Ollama","sectionNumber":"1.7","sectionTitle":"Python scripts with Ollama","content":"1.7 Python scripts with Ollama\nWe can use a Python script to programmatically interact with the local Ollama LLM model.\nThe AI LLM Landscape and Key Parameters 26\nGetting ready\nUse the existing Python virtual environment or create a new one and install the ollama Python\nlibrary:\n$ python3 -m venv .venv\n$ source .venv/bin/activate\n$ pip install ollama\nHow to do it…\n1. Construct a simple Python library, example_1.py:\nfrom ollama import Client\nclient = Client(host='http://localhost:11434')\nresponse = client.chat(model='gemma3:1b', messages=[\n{\n'role': 'user',\n'content': 'What is Layer 2 of the OSI model?',\n},\n])\nprint(response['message']['content'])\n2. Execute and observe the output:\n$ python example_1.py\nLayer 2 of the OSI model is the **Data Link Layer**. It's the layer\nclosest to the user and responsible for reliable transfer of data\nbetween two directly connected nodes – like your computer and your\nrouter – within a local network (like your home or office network).\nHere's a breakdown of what it does and its key functions:\n**1. Focus:** Reliable data delivery over a physical link.\n**2. Key Functions:**\n* **Physical Addressing:** Layer 2 uses MAC (Media Access Control)\naddresses to identify devices on the local network. This allows\ndevices to communicate directly without needing to understand the IP","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":54,"pageEnd":55,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::44","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"Chapter 1 27","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 1 27\naddress.\n* **Error Detection and Correction:** It implements mechanisms to\ndetect and correct errors that occur during transmission, ensuring\ndata integrity.\n* **Framing:** It packages data into frames, which are the basic\nunits of transmission over a network. This includes things like\nheader and trailer information.\n* **Logical Addressing:** While it doesn't have IP addresses, it\nuses MAC addresses for identification and manages the flow of data\nwithin a network segment.\n* **Media Access Control (MAC) Protocols:** This is a *critical*\nfunction. Layer 2 protocols like Ethernet and Wi-Fi dictate how\ndevices on the local network share the network medium. It manages\ncollisions and ensures that devices don't interfere with each other.\nReplace the content with other network engineering related\nquestions.\nLet’s see how the script works.\nHow it works…\nThe script demonstrates how to interact with a local AI Ollama server. It uses the ollama Python\nlibrary to query the gemma3:1b model we installed. We initiated the client object to communicate\nwith the server on port 11434.\nThe client.chat method is used to send a conversation to the AI model and receive a response.\nThere’s more…\nCheck out https://github.com/ollama/ollama-python for more information on the Ollama\nPython library.\nThe AI LLM Landscape and Key Parameters 28\nSummary\nIn this chapter, we took steps to set up our development environment and experiment with\nOpenAI and Ollama. We started with basic setups and progressively moved toward more options.\nWe experimented with some of the features to see the difference in responses. We were also able\nto construct simple Python scripts to pragmatically retrieve responses from our AI models. The\namount of information might feel a bit overwhelming at first, due to the scope of topics we covered.\nBut keep in mind that this is generally due to the fact that this chapter provides a foundation for\nfuture chapters.\nIn the next chapter, we will turn our attention to focus more on OpenAI and its LLM models.\nGet This Book’s PDF Version and\nExclusive Extras\nScan the QR code (or go to packtpub.com/unlock). Search for this\nbook by name, confirm the edition, and then follow the steps on\nthe page.\nNote: Keep your invoice handy. Purchases made directly from Packt\ndon’t require an invoice.\n2\nOpenAI Recipes for Network\nEngineers\nIn Chapter 1, we discussed various ways to set up our environment, including a development\nenvironment for OpenAI, the company that many perceive as having started the AI large lan-\nguage model (LLM) revolution. OpenAI’s LLM offers network engineers a powerful toolkit for\nautomating routine tasks, generating topology, configuring devices, and providing expert-level\nassistance in many other areas.\nIn this chapter, we will dive deeper into using OpenAI’s models to help us work on tasks within","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":56,"pageEnd":58,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::45","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"Chapter 1 27","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"the network engineering context.\nThis chapter provides five recipes that progressively build your expertise in leveraging OpenAI’s\ncapabilities for network engineering tasks. We will experiment with different tools, such as curl\nand Postman, as well as leveraging OpenAI’s test-to-image capabilities.\nSpecifically, we will cover the following recipes:\n• Using curl for OpenAI API communications\n• Using Postman with the OpenAI API\n• Fine-tuning OpenAI responses\n• Generating network topology with OpenAI\n• Generating methods of procedure (MOPs) with OpenAI\nOpenAI Recipes for Network Engineers 30\nAs we saw in Chapter 1, OpenAI’s offerings are cloud-based; they have never officially provided\nlocally hosted versions of their modern models, such as GPT-3 or GPT-4. They did, at one point,\nprovide an open source version of the model, GPT-2, that can be run locally, but this is an older,\nmuch more limited model compared to their current offerings. Therefore, in this chapter, we will\nfocus on the cloud-based offerings.\nTechnical requirements\nThis chapter builds on the recipes provided in Chapter 1, where we set up an OpenAI environment\nby completing tasks such as signing up for an account, generating an API key, and understanding\nthe different parameters. If you are unfamiliar with them, please review them before moving on\nto this chapter’s recipes.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":58,"pageEnd":59,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::46","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"2.1","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"2.1 Using curl for OpenAI API communication","sectionNumber":"2.1","sectionTitle":"Using curl for OpenAI API communication","content":"2.1 Using curl for OpenAI API communication\ncurl (https://github.com/curl/curl) is one of the most ubiquitous command-line tools when\nit comes to testing API communications. It allows us to learn the fundamentals of API communi-\ncation with OpenAI, such as establishing connectivity, handling authentication, and processing\nresponses directly in the command-line interface (CLI).\nGetting ready\nFor many Linux distributions, curl is installed by default. We can check whether that is the case\nwith the curl command:\n$ curl --version\ncurl 8.5.0 (x86_64-pc-linux-gnu) libcurl/8.5.0 OpenSSL/3.0.13 zlib/1.3\nbrotli/1.1.0 zstd/1.5.5 libidn2/2.3.7 libpsl/0.21.2 (+libidn2/2.3.7)\nlibssh/0.10.6/openssl/zlib nghttp2/1.59.0 librtmp/2.3 OpenLDAP/2.6.7\nHowever, if it is not already installed, you can find the installation instructions at https://curl.\nse/docs/install.html.\nAdditionally, make sure you obtain OpenAI keys and have them ready to go (see the Setting up an\nOpenAI environment and Python scripts with OpenAI recipes in Chapter 1).\nHow to do it…\n1. Export your OpenAI API key as an environment variable:\nexport OPENAI_API_KEY=\"your-openai-api-key\"","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":59,"pageEnd":59,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::47","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"Chapter 2 31","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 2 31\n2. Let’s make an API call to list all the available models from OpenAI:\ncurl -v https://api.openai.com/v1/models -H \"Authorization: Bearer\n$OPENAI_API_KEY\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\"\nWe should see a list of available models in the response. Your output might be different\nif different models are on offer at the time of execution:\n$ curl -v https://api.openai.com/v1/models -H \"Authorization:\nBearer $OPENAI_API_KEY\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\"\n* Host api.openai.com:443 was resolved.\n* IPv6: (none)\n* IPv4: 162.159.140.245, 172.66.0.243\n* Trying 162.159.140.245:443...\n* Connected to api.openai.com (162.159.140.245) port 443\n* ALPN: curl offers h2,http/1.1\n…\n<\n{\n\"object\": \"list\",\n\"data\": [\n{\n\"id\": \"gpt-4-0613\",\n\"object\": \"model\",\n\"created\": 1686588896,\n\"owned_by\": \"openai\"\n},\n{\n\"id\": \"gpt-4\",\n\"object\": \"model\",\n\"created\": 1687882411,\n\"owned_by\": \"openai\"\n},\n{\n\"id\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\",\n\"object\": \"model\",\n\"created\": 1677610602,\n\"owned_by\": \"openai\"\n},\nOpenAI Recipes for Network Engineers 32\n…\n{\n\"id\": \"ft:gpt-3.5-turbo-0125:personal::9FZ8Mncg\",\n\"object\": \"model\",\n\"created\": 1713497002,\n\"owned_by\": \"network-automation-nerds\"\n}\n]\n* Connection #0 to host api.openai.com left intact\nRemember how we were able to influence OpenAI’s response via system messages, user\nprompts, and temperature? We can also use them with curl:\n$ curl -X POST \"https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions\" \\\n-H \"Authorization: Bearer $OPENAI_API_KEY\" \\\n-H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n-d '{\n\"model\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\",\n\"messages\": [\n{\n\"role\": \"system\",\n\"content\": \"You are a senior network engineer with 15 years\nof experience. Provide technical, accurate responses with\npractical examples.\"\n},\n{\n\"role\": \"user\",\n\"content\": \"Explain the difference between TCP and UDP,\nincluding when to use each in network applications.\"\n}],\n\"temperature\": 0.3\n}\nWe should receive a response similar to the one below:\n{\n\"id\": \"chatcmpl-BvElfOhcgv48MSQzT90KFcS65Ykuk\",\n\"object\": \"chat.completion\",\n\"created\": 1752981163,\n\"model\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo-0125\",","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":60,"pageEnd":61,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::48","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"Chapter 2 33","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 2 33\n\"choices\": [\n{\n\"index\": 0,\n\"message\": {\n\"role\": \"assistant\",\n\"content\": \"TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) and\nUDP (User Datagram Protocol) are two of the most commonly used\ntransport layer protocols in computer networks. Here are the key\ndifferences between TCP and UDP:\\n\\n1. **Connection-Oriented vs.\nConnectionless**: TCP is connection-oriented, meaning it establishes\na connection between the sender and receiver before data transfer\nbegins. UDP, on the other hand, is connectionless, where data can\nbe sent without prior communication.\\n\\n2. **Reliability**: TCP\nprovides reliable, error-checked delivery of data. It ensures that\ndata is received in the correct order and retransmits any lost\npackets. UDP, on the other hand, does not guarantee delivery or\norder of packets. It is considered unreliable but is faster due\nto less overhead.\\n\\n3. **Flow Control and Congestion Control**:\nTCP includes mechanisms for flow control and congestion control to\nmanage the rate of data transmission and prevent network congestion.\nUDP does not have built-in flow or congestion control mechanisms.\\n\\\nn4. **Header Size**:\",\n\"refusal\": null,\n\"annotations\": []\n},\n\"logprobs\": null,\n\"finish_reason\": \"length\"\n}\n…\n3. For interactive applications or long responses, we want to use the streaming and no-buffer\noptions:\n$ curl -X POST \"https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions\" -H\n\"Authorization: Bearer $OPENAI_API_KEY\" -H \"Content-Type:\napplication/json\" -d '{\n\"model\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\",\n\"messages\": [\n{\n\"role\": \"system\",\nOpenAI Recipes for Network Engineers 34\n\"content\": \"You are creating a comprehensive network\nsecurity checklist.\"\n},\n{\n\"role\": \"user\",\n\"content\": \"Create a detailed network security audit\nchecklist for a mid-size company with 500 employees,\ncovering perimeter security, internal network segmentation,\nand monitoring.\"\n}\n],\n\"max_tokens\": 800\n}' --no-buffer 0.3\nThe response is too long to display here, but you should see more streaming output than what\nwas shown in previous examples.\nHow it works…\nIn step 1, as a security best practice, we exported our OpenAI API key and created it as an environ-\nment variable. This stores the OPENAI_API_KEY variable for the current shell session. If we start\nanother shell session, we will need to create the environment variable again.\nIn step 2, we sent an HTTP GET request to https://api.openai.com/v1/models. The -v switch\nturns on verbose mode, which gives us more information during our learning session. The /v1/\nmodels endpoint lists all available OpenAI models, while -H specifies HTTP headers to be included\nin our request. In this case, we used Authorization: Bearer to indicate the HTTP header for au-\nthentication and $OPENAI_API_KEY to specify the environment variable key that will be substituted\nwith the actual value when we make the request. We also used Content-Type: application/","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":62,"pageEnd":63,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::49","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"Chapter 2 33","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"json, which tells the server that we are sending the data in JSON format. Finally, max-time 30\nsets the maximum time allowed for the operation. This is mainly as a precaution, to prevent the\nsession from hanging if there is a network issue that causes delays.\nIf you’re using a Windows command prompt, please use set OPENAI_API_\nKEY=\"your-api-key\".","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":63,"pageEnd":63,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::50","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"Chapter 2 35","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 2 35\nIn step 3, we sent an HTTP POST request to https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions with\nthe same headers of authorization and content-type. However, because it is a POST request, we\ncan now include a body with the request. This body includes the model and messages. Within the\nmessage, we are able to specify the system message, the user message, the maximum number of\ntokens, and the temperature. These parameters were covered in an earlier recipe 1.2 in Chapter 1.\nIn step 4, we anticipated that the response would be long. In a traditional API call, the server would\nhave gathered all the answers and responded all at once. For longer responses, this can take up to\n30 seconds. By using the streams: true option, we were able to see the response immediately,\nwhich allowed for a better user experience.\nThere’s more…\ncurl is a mature and powerful command-line tool. We only covered the basics in this recipe. If\nyou’re interested, please feel free to investigate the following topics:\n• Advanced curl features such as config for complex configurations, retry for retry logic,\nand saving responses to a file with -o\n• Using shell scripts for error handling, logging, and batch processing\n• Using jq (https://jqlang.org/) for JSON output manipulation","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":64,"pageEnd":64,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::51","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"2.2","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"2.2 Using Postman with the OpenAI API","sectionNumber":"2.2","sectionTitle":"Using Postman with the OpenAI API","content":"2.2 Using Postman with the OpenAI API\nWhen it comes to API testing, curl is great at stripping away all the non-essential items, such as the\ngraphical user interface. But once we are familiar with the basic process, we might want to move\non to more polished tools with more refined user interfaces. After all, typing in command-line\noptions every single time is probably not your favorite activity in the day.\nPostman (https://www.postman.com/) is another popular tool for working with APIs. It elevates\nyour API testing capabilities by introducing a user-friendly graphical interface. Postman provides\na professional-grade interface for API testing, development, and collaboration.\nThis recipe covers the basic features for using Postman with the OpenAI API, such as environmental\nvariable management, automated testing, and team collaboration.\nOpenAI Recipes for Network Engineers 36\nGetting ready\nTo use Postman, we need to either use the Postman web-based interface or download the Postman\napplication specific to the operating system we are using. There are free as well as paid tiers. To\nuse the free tier, we will need to sign up for a free account. If you do not have an account, please\ncreate one at https://www.postman.com/postman-account/.\nThe web interface and application interface are fairly similar by design.\nFigure 2.1: Postman web interface","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":64,"pageEnd":65,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::52","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"Chapter 2 37","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 2 37\nHere is a glance at the Postman macOS application (Windows is similar):\nFigure 2.2: Postman macOS application\nI find the Postman application interface easier to work with. Therefore, in this recipe, I will use the\nPostman app for demonstration purposes. Please feel free to use the web interface if you prefer.\nWe also need our OpenAI API key ready to go. Please refer to the recipe in Chapter 1 if you do not\nalready have one.\nOpenAI Recipes for Network Engineers 38\nHow to do it…\nWe’ll start by creating a dedicated workspace for this recipe:\n1. Click on the top navigation menu on the left-hand side, choose Workspaces, and choose\nto create a new workspace. There are several templates to pick from, but we’ll pick Blank\nworkspace to begin with. I will name the workspace Chapter 2. OpenAI Testing with\nPostman.\nFigure 2.3: Creating a new workspace","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":66,"pageEnd":67,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::53","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"Chapter 2 39","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 2 39\n2. Create an environment by clicking on Environments in the left sidebar and picking Create\nEnvironment. Once created, we can use the triple dot to rename it. In this example, we\nwill name this collection OpenAI Network Engineering – Testing.\nFigure 2.4: Creating an environment\nOpenAI Recipes for Network Engineers 40\n3. Create a new collection by selecting Collections from the left menu. Ensure you are in the\nenvironment we created by checking the top left-hand corner.\nFigure 2.5: Creating a collection\n4. In the collection, we can save time by creating an authorization header for OPENAI_API_KEY\nsince we need to use it for every single API request (the security warning shown in the\nfollowing figure is a reminder for production use; there are more secure ways to store\nsensitive data, such as vault).","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":68,"pageEnd":69,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::54","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"Chapter 2 41","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 2 41\nFigure 2.6: Adding an authorization header\n5. We can also create a global variable that we can reference in our individual requests. Navi-\ngate to Variables and create a base_url variable with a value of https://api.openai.com.\nMake sure you click on the Save button at the top to save the changes.\nFigure 2.7: Collection variables\nOpenAI Recipes for Network Engineers 42\n6. Click Add a request.\nFigure 2.8: Adding requests\n7. For our first request, we can use a simple GET request to see a list of models. We’ll name\nthe request model lists and use {{base_url}} to reference the collection-level variable\nfor the base URL, then append /v1/models to complete the full path. Since we entered the\nAPI key at the collection level, we can simply click Send and receive the response.\nFigure 2.9: Our first GET request","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":70,"pageEnd":71,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::55","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"Chapter 2 43","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 2 43\n8. Let’s go ahead and make another request. For this request, we want to change the type\nto POST and set the endpoint to {{base_url}}/v1/chat/completions. In this example,\nchat completion is being used as the name of the request.\nFigure 2.10: Chat completion request\n9. Now, we need to add an additional header for Content-Type.\nFigure 2.11: New chat completion header\n10. Under Body, we will use raw and JSON as our formats. Type in the same context we used\npreviously for chat completion.\nFigure 2.12: New chat completion request body\nOpenAI Recipes for Network Engineers 44\n11. If everything has been entered correctly, after clicking Send, we should be able to see the\nresponse we saw previously.\nFigure 2.13: New chat completion response\nHow it works…\nThis recipe is pretty straightforward, as we did not introduce any new elements to our request\nfrom when we used curl. The header, the body, and the responses were identical. What we did\nintroduce is the new Postman interface, which is more user-friendly and streamlines our API calls\ninto a hierarchical structure. We used environments to separate our high-level environments,\nsuch as testing, QA, and production. We also used collections to separate and group different\nAPI calls into their logical use cases.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":72,"pageEnd":73,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::56","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"Chapter 2 45","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 2 45\nPostman provides an easy way to inherit headers, such as the OPENAI_API_KEY authentication\nheader, from a collection for individual requests, thus saving us time and reducing the chance of\nerrors. The interface also allows us to easily modify the request body if we need to modify various\nparameters, such as the model type or temperature, without needing to copy and paste or type\nin the other unchanged parameters.\nWhen OpenAI processes our request, it returns an HTTP response with a status code of 200 for\nsuccess, 401 for authentication issues, and 500 for server errors. With syntax highlighting, trou-\nbleshooting issues also becomes easier.\nThere’s more…\nPostman is very useful when it comes to API testing. For additional challenges, feel free to create\nmore environments, such as production and QA. Within those environments, create separate\ncollections as applicable.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":74,"pageEnd":74,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::57","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"2.3","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"2.3 Fine-tuning OpenAI responses","sectionNumber":"2.3","sectionTitle":"Fine-tuning OpenAI responses","content":"2.3 Fine-tuning OpenAI responses\nThe OpenAI LLM base model, sometimes referred to as the foundation model, is amazingly func-\ntional and can handle most, if not all, the tasks we throw at it. As we have seen so far, it can help us\nwith many general network engineering tasks, such as configuring VLANs, assigning IP addresses,\nand changing interface descriptions.\nBut one thing it does not currently do, as no out-of-the-box solution can do, is personalize the\nfeedback for our specific organizational needs. For example, if our team wants to always use\nLoopback 0 for router-id when we configure BGP, the foundation model would not know that.\nWe can customize our OpenAI model for our specific needs by fine-tuning it. Fine-tuning allows us\nto teach OpenAI models our organization’s specific network configuration standard, for example,\nso that we do not just get the generic configurations.\nIn this recipe, we will learn how to prepare training data, execute the fine-tuning process, and\nvalidate results to ensure the model reflects our company’s standardized procedures.\nGetting ready\nPlease review the Setting up an OpenAI environment recipe in Chapter 1, which specifies how to\nuse a Python virtual environment, get an OpenAI API key, and install the openai Python package.\nOpenAI Recipes for Network Engineers 46\nHow to do it…\n1. We will start by querying the default OpenAI model to generate a BGP neighbor configu -\nration. We will name this script recipe_3_01_test_base_model.py:\nimport os\nfrom openai import OpenAI\napi_key = os.getenv('OPENAI_API_KEY')\nclient = OpenAI(api_key=api_key)\nquery = \"Configure BGP for AS 65001 with neighbor 192.168.1.2 in","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":74,"pageEnd":75,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::58","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"2.3","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"AS 65002\"","sectionNumber":"2.3","sectionTitle":"Fine-tuning OpenAI responses","content":"AS 65002\"\nresponse = client.chat.completions.create(\nmodel=\"gpt-3.5-turbo\",\nmessages=[\n{\n\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You are a network engineer\nGenerate BGP configuration.\"\n},\n{\n\"role\": \"user\",\n\"content\": query\n}\n],\nmax_tokens=200,\ntemperature=0.1\n)\nprint(\"BASE MODEL OUTPUT:\")\nprint(response.choices[0].message.content)\n2. The result is a generic BGP configuration. There’s nothing wrong with it, but it is just\nabout as basic as it gets:\n$ python recipe_3_01_test_base_model.py","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":75,"pageEnd":75,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::59","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"2.3","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"BASE MODEL OUTPUT:","sectionNumber":"2.3","sectionTitle":"Fine-tuning OpenAI responses","content":"BASE MODEL OUTPUT:\n```","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":75,"pageEnd":75,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::60","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"Chapter 2 47","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 2 47\nrouter bgp 65001\nneighbor 192.168.1.2 remote-as 65002\n```\n3. Let’s use a script to generate some training data. We will name this script recipe_3_02_\ncreate_training_data.py. At the time of writing, OpenAI requires at least 10 examples\nof training data. The output is saved in a file named bgp_training_data.jsonl:\nimport json\ntraining_examples = [\n{\n\"messages\": [\n{\n\"role\": \"system\",\n\"content\": \"You are a network engineer. Always use\nLoopback0 for BGP router-id and enable\nlog-neighbor-changes.\"},\n{\n\"role\": \"user\",\n\"content\": \"Configure BGP for AS 65001 with\nneighbor 192.168.1.2 in AS 65002\"\n},\n{\n\"role\": \"assistant\",\n\"content\": \"interface Loopback0\\n description\nBGP_ROUTER_ID\\n\nip address 10.1.1.1 255.255.255.255\\n!\\nrouter\nbgp 65001\\n bgp router-id 10.1.1.1\\n\nbgp log-neighbor-changes\\n\nneighbor 192.168.1.2 remote-as 65002\\n\nneighbor 192.168.1.2 activate\"\n}\n]\n},\n{\n\"messages\": [\n{\nOpenAI Recipes for Network Engineers 48\n\"role\": \"system\",\n\"content\": \"You are a network engineer.\nAlways use Loopback0 for\nBGP router-id and enable log-neighbor-changes.\"\n},\n{\n\"role\": \"user\",\n\"content\": \"Set up BGP for AS 65010 with\nneighbor 10.0.1.1 in AS 65020\"\n},\n{\n\"role\": \"assistant\",\n\"content\": \"interface Loopback0\\n\ndescription BGP_ROUTER_ID\\n\nip address 10.10.1.1 255.255.255.255\\n!\\nrouter\nbgp 65010\\n bgp router-id 10.10.1.1\\n bgp\nlog-neighbor-changes\\n neighbor 10.0.1.1\nremote-as 65020\\n neighbor 10.0.1.1 activate\"\n}\n]\n},\n…outoput committed…\n{\n\"role\": \"user\",\n\"content\": \"Setup BGP routing for AS 66500 connecting to","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":76,"pageEnd":77,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::61","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"203.0.113.254","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"203.0.113.254 AS 66600\"","sectionNumber":"203.0.113.254","sectionTitle":"AS 66600\"","content":"203.0.113.254 AS 66600\"\n},\n{\n\"role\": \"assistant\",\n\"content\": \"interface Loopback0\\n description\nBGP_ROUTER_ID\\n ip address\n10.65.1.1 255.255.255.255\\n!\\nrouter bgp 66500\\n\nbgp router-id 10.65.1.1\\n bgp log-neighbor-changes\\n\nneighbor 203.0.113.254 remote-as 66600\\n\nneighbor 203.0.113.254 activate\"\n}\n]","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":77,"pageEnd":77,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::62","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"Chapter 2 49","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 2 49\n}\n]\nwith open('bgp_training_data.jsonl', 'w') as f:\nfor example in training_examples:\nf.write(json.dumps(example) + '\\n')\nprint(\"Training data created: bgp_training_data.jsonl\")\nprint(f\"Total examples: {len(training_examples)}\")\n4. Go ahead and run the script we created in step 3 and observe the result.\n5. We will use the recipe_3_03_create_fine_tuned_model.py script to create the new\nfine-tuned model:\nimport os\nimport time\nfrom openai import OpenAI\napi_key = os.getenv('OPENAI_API_KEY')\nclient = OpenAI(api_key=api_key)\nprint(\"Uploading training data...\")\nwith open('bgp_training_data.jsonl', 'rb') as f:\nfile_response = client.files.create(file=f, purpose='fine-tune')\nprint(f\"File uploaded: {file_response.id}\")\nprint(\"Starting fine-tuning job...\")\njob = client.fine_tuning.jobs.create(\ntraining_file=file_response.id,\nmodel=\"gpt-3.5-turbo\",\nsuffix=\"bgp-standards\"\n)\nprint(f\"Fine-tuning job created: {job.id}\")\nprint(\"Waiting for completion...\")\nwhile True:\nOpenAI Recipes for Network Engineers 50\njob_status = client.fine_tuning.jobs.retrieve(job.id)\nif job_status.status == \"succeeded\":\nprint(\nf\"Fine-tuning completed: {job_status.fine_tuned_model}\"\n)\nwith open('fine_tuned_model_id.txt', 'w') as f:\nf.write(job_status.fine_tuned_model)\nbreak\nelif job_status.status in [\"failed\", \"cancelled\"]:\nprint(f\"Fine-tuning {job_status.status}\")\nbreak\ntime.sleep(30)\n6. When we execute this script, it will take anywhere between 10 and 30 minutes to complete.\nOnce finished, we will receive a model ID for our fine-tuned model. Save this ID in a file\nnamed fine_tuned_model_id.txt so that it can be used in the next step:\n$ python3 recipe_3_03_create_fine_tuned_model.py\nUploading training data...\nFile uploaded: file-QFBjPJCse6L9zsZfbtan5A\nStarting fine-tuning job...\nFine-tuning job created: ftjob-TlSBbLCQPxyQ2NS6qqQfHRSa\nWaiting for completion...\n[note. This will take a while]\nFine-tuning completed: ft:gpt-3.5-turbo-0125:network-automation-\nnerds:bgp-standards:BvTBFsUu","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":78,"pageEnd":79,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf::63","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ainetworkingcookbook ainetworkingcookbook","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","fileName":"ainetworkingcookbook.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"ainetworkingcookbook","heading":"Chapter 2 51","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 2 51\n7. We can view the progress and the success/failure of fine-tuned models via OpenAI’s con -\nsole.\nFigure 2.14: OpenAI fine-tuned models\n8. We will use the recipe_3_04_test_fine_tuned_model.py script to query the new fine-\ntuned model:\nimport os\nfrom openai import OpenAI\napi_key = os.getenv('OPENAI_API_KEY')\nclient = OpenAI(api_key=api_key)\nwith open('fine_tuned_model_id.txt', 'r') as f:\nfine_tuned_model = f.read().strip()\nquery = \"Configure BGP for AS 65001 with neighbor\n192.168.1.2 in AS 65002\"\nresponse = client.chat.completions.create(\nmodel=fine_tuned_model,\nmessages=[\n{\n\"role\": \"system\",\nOpenAI Recipes for Network Engineers 52\n\"content\": \"You are a network engineer. Generate BGP\nconfiguration.\"\n},\n{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": query}\n],\nmax_tokens=200,\ntemperature=0.1\n)\nprint(\"FINE-TUNED MODEL OUTPUT:\")\nprint(response.choices[0].message.content)\n9. Just like magic, the new result should reflect the fine-tuned result we implemented with\nour training data, including the interface configuration and additional BGP parameters!\n$ python recipe_3_04_test_fine_tuned_model.py","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":80,"pageEnd":81,"embeddingF32":""}]}